title: What is Cloud Security?
page: what-is-cloud-security.html
as-of: 2026-11-25
promise: The provider secures the platform, you secure how you configure and use it, and almost every notable cloud breach comes down to a customer-side failure.

== The 60-second version
layout: text
section: top
Cloud security protects data, identities, and workloads on AWS, Azure, GCP, and similar platforms. The provider secures the platform; you secure how you configure and use it. Most real breaches come from misconfigurations, over-privileged IAM, stolen credentials, or insecure APIs.
notes:
Cloud security protects data, identities and workloads running on AWS, Azure, GCP and similar platforms. The provider secures the underlying platform, and you secure how you configure and use it.

The page says most real breaches come from misconfigurations, over-privileged IAM, stolen credentials or insecure APIs, rather than exotic zero-days. The toolkit is built around posture management, workload protection, entitlement analysis and, increasingly, all-in-one CNAPP platforms.

== What cloud security is
layout: text
section: definition
Cloud security protects the data, applications, identities and infrastructure on cloud platforms. That is primarily AWS, Azure and GCP, plus second-tier providers and the SaaS layer. It is not a single product or a single team.
notes:
Cloud security is the practice of protecting data, applications, identities and infrastructure that run on cloud platforms. That primarily means AWS, Azure and Google Cloud, but it also covers second-tier providers like Oracle Cloud, IBM Cloud and Alibaba Cloud, and the SaaS layer: Microsoft 365, Salesforce, Snowflake, GitHub and so on.

It is not a single product or a single team. In a mature organization it spans configuration management, identity and access, network controls, data protection, application security, threat detection, incident response and compliance, across hundreds or thousands of accounts, subscriptions and projects.

== The shared responsibility model
layout: compare
section: shared-responsibility
col: Security of the cloud | Entirely the provider's job | Data centers, network backbone, hypervisor | Managed-service internals
col: Security in the cloud | Your job | Configurations and IAM policies | Code, data, network rules, workloads
The line moves depending on the service.
notes:
The page calls the shared responsibility model the most important concept in cloud security. Cloud providers secure some things and you secure the rest.

Security of the cloud is entirely the provider's job: physical data centers, the network backbone, the hypervisor and the managed-service internals. Security in the cloud is yours: your configurations, IAM policies, code, data, network rules and the workloads you deploy. Where the line sits depends on the service model, which the next slide covers.

== Where the line sits
layout: table
section: shared-responsibility
row: Model | The provider | You still own
row: IaaS, such as EC2 | Hypervisor and below | OS patching, network rules, IAM, applications, data
row: PaaS, such as RDS | Patches the runtime | Configuration, access controls, data
row: SaaS, such as Microsoft 365 | Runs the application | User accounts, data classification, sharing settings, integrations
Cloud Run is different: it runs your container image, so its OS packages are yours to patch, unless you deploy on Google's buildpack base images with automatic base image updates turned on.
notes:
With IaaS, raw VMs such as EC2, you secure almost everything above the hypervisor: OS patching, network rules, IAM, applications and data.

With PaaS, managed services such as RDS and App Service, the provider patches the runtime, but you still own configuration, access controls and data. Cloud Run is different, because it runs the container image you deploy. The OS packages in that image are yours to patch, unless you deploy on Google's buildpack base images with automatic base image updates turned on.

With SaaS, such as Microsoft 365 or Salesforce, the provider runs the application. You still own user accounts, data classification, sharing settings and integrations.

== Whose failure is it?
layout: text
section: shared-responsibility
Almost every notable cloud breach comes down to a customer-side failure rather than a provider compromise. Capital One, Snowflake/UNC5537 and LastPass are instructive. Microsoft Storm-0558 is the instructive exception: a stolen Microsoft signing key and a Microsoft token-validation flaw.
notes:
The page says almost every notable cloud breach comes down to a customer-side failure rather than a provider compromise. The Capital One, Snowflake/UNC5537 and LastPass incidents are all instructive.

Microsoft Storm-0558 is the instructive exception: a stolen Microsoft signing key and a Microsoft token-validation flaw. The page points to the breach kill chains for full step-by-step analyses.

== The core pillars
layout: list
section: pillars
item: Identity and access
item: Configuration and posture
item: Network security
item: Data protection
item: Application and workload security
item: Detection and response
item: Compliance and governance
You can carve up cloud security a dozen ways. These are the pillars most teams organize around.
notes:
The page says you can carve up cloud security a dozen ways, and these seven pillars are the ones most teams organize around: identity and access, configuration and posture, network security, data protection, application and workload security, detection and response, and compliance and governance.

They are in the page's order, and the page does not rank them. The next slides take them in turn.

== Identity is the new perimeter
layout: checklist
section: pillars
check: Least-privilege policies
check: No long-lived access keys where you can avoid them
check: MFA everywhere
check: Continuous review of who can do what
Every API call is authenticated and authorized against an identity.
notes:
In the cloud, every API call is authenticated and authorized against an identity: a user, a role, a service principal or a workload identity. Strong IAM means least-privilege policies, no long-lived access keys where you can avoid them, MFA everywhere, and continuous review of who can do what.

Most cloud breaches involve at least one identity failure: an over-privileged role, a leaked key or a phished session token.

== Configuration and posture
layout: text
section: pillars
The cloud is a giant configuration surface. Day-to-day failure modes: public S3 buckets, open security groups, unencrypted disks, disabled logging, weak password policies. CSPM tools find them at scale and keep them fixed.
notes:
The cloud is a giant configuration surface. Its day-to-day failure modes are public S3 buckets, open security groups, unencrypted disks, disabled logging and weak password policies. CSPM tools exist to find them at scale and keep them fixed.

== Network, data and workloads
layout: table
section: pillars
row: Pillar | What it covers
row: Network | VPCs, subnets, security groups, private endpoints; provider-specific and always changing
row: Data | Encrypt at rest and in transit, manage keys, classify, stop exfiltration
row: Workloads | Code, dependency scanning, container hardening, runtime protection, CI/CD
notes:
For network security the building blocks are VPCs, subnets, security groups, network ACLs, private endpoints, transit gateways and service meshes. The principles of segmenting, restricting and monitoring are familiar, but the page says the implementation is provider-specific and changes constantly.

For data protection, encrypt data at rest and in transit, manage keys in KMS, Key Vault or Cloud KMS, classify what is sensitive and prevent exfiltration. Increasingly this also means thinking about what data you let LLMs and AI services see.

For application and workload security, the page lists secure code, dependency scanning, container image hardening, runtime protection for VMs and Kubernetes, serverless function security and the CI/CD pipeline. CWPP and CNAPP platforms cover much of this.

== Detection, response and compliance
layout: compare
section: pillars
col: Detection and response | CloudTrail, Activity Log, Audit Logs into a SIEM | GuardDuty, Defender for Cloud, Security Command Center | A response runbook
col: Compliance and governance | SOC 2, ISO 27001, PCI DSS, HIPAA, FedRAMP, GDPR, NIS2 | Did you implement the controls? Can you prove it?
Detection content maps to MITRE ATT&CK Cloud techniques.
notes:
For detection, the pieces are CloudTrail, Azure Activity Log and GCP Audit Logs feeding a SIEM; provider-native services such as GuardDuty, Defender for Cloud and Security Command Center; detection content mapped to MITRE ATT&CK Cloud techniques; and a response runbook for when something fires.

For compliance, the page lists SOC 2, ISO 27001, PCI DSS, HIPAA, FedRAMP, GDPR and the NIS2 Directive. Most compliance work is answering two questions: did you implement the controls, and can you prove it? The proof mostly comes from configuration evidence the cloud already produces.

== The threats that matter
layout: list
section: threats
item: Misconfiguration
item: Over-privileged identities
item: Credential theft
item: Insecure APIs and SSRF
item: Supply-chain compromise
item: Account takeover
item: Insider risk
Real breaches tend to follow these repeatable patterns, plus AI/LLM-specific risks such as prompt injection.
notes:
The page says the breaches that happen in practice tend to come from a small set of repeatable patterns, and names eight. Misconfiguration means public buckets, exposed databases, open admin endpoints and missing logging. For over-privileged identities the page's example is Capital One, where a WAF role could list and read S3 buckets it had no business touching. Credential theft covers phishing, info-stealers, hardcoded secrets and leaked CI tokens, with Snowflake/UNC5537 as the example. Insecure APIs and SSRF mean apps coerced into calling internal endpoints, including the metadata service.

Supply-chain compromise means a poisoned dependency, a compromised CI step or a malicious GitHub Action, with SolarWinds as the example. Account takeover covers MFA fatigue, session theft and helpdesk social engineering, with Scattered Spider and MGM as the example. Insider risk covers departing engineers, over-broad access and audit gaps no one was watching.

The eighth pattern is AI and LLM-specific risk: prompt injection, data leakage via outputs, and agents with too much authority.

== Defense in depth
layout: figure
section: threats
figure: dd-title
Real attacks chain several of these threats together.
notes:
The figure shows defense in depth: six concentric layers from the outermost perimeter to the innermost data, each with its own controls. Its caption says one control per layer is brittle, and that you should stack controls so a single failure does not reach the data.

The page also says real attacks chain several threats together, and points to the breach kill chains, mapped to MITRE ATT&CK Cloud techniques, to see exactly how.

== The tool landscape
layout: figure
section: tools
figure: cnapp-umbrella
CNAPP bundles CSPM, CWPP, CIEM, DSPM and IaC scanning.
notes:
The figure shows CNAPP as the umbrella category that bundles CSPM, CWPP, CIEM, DSPM and IaC scanning into a single platform. Its caption says CNAPP bundles the previously separate categories into one platform, and that the market is consolidating fast.

== What each category does
layout: table
section: tools
row: Category | What it does
row: CSPM | Continuously scans accounts for misconfigurations against a benchmark like CIS or your own policies
row: CWPP | Protects running VMs, containers and serverless functions
row: CIEM | Analyzes IAM: who can assume which role, which permissions go unused
row: DSPM | Finds and classifies sensitive data, then tracks who can access it
row: SIEM/SOAR | Centralizes logs and automates response
notes:
CSPM continuously scans your cloud accounts for misconfigurations against a benchmark like CIS or your own policies. Examples the page names are Prowler, ScoutSuite and Steampipe, plus commercial platforms.

CWPP protects running workloads, meaning VMs, containers and serverless functions, with vulnerability scanning, runtime detection and file integrity monitoring. CIEM analyzes IAM at scale: who can assume which role, which permissions go unused and where the privilege-escalation paths are.

DSPM finds and classifies sensitive data across cloud stores and tracks who can access it. SIEM and SOAR centralize logs and automate response, and the page says the cloud-native variants matter more as log volume explodes.

CNAPP is the umbrella that bundles CSPM, CWPP and CIEM, and often vulnerability management, container security and IaC scanning. The page also lists provider-native services as often the cheapest credible starting point: AWS GuardDuty, Security Hub and IAM Access Analyzer; Microsoft Defender for Cloud with Entra; and GCP Security Command Center.

== How to get started
layout: checklist
section: getting-started
check: Pick one cloud
check: Learn IAM and networking first
check: Practice on intentionally vulnerable environments
check: Read real breach post-mortems
check: Pick up one foundational certification
check: Join a community
This order works for most people.
notes:
The page says this order works for most people. Pick one cloud: AWS, Azure or GCP, and do not try to learn three at once. AWS has the deepest community and the most learning material.

Learn IAM and networking first, because almost every breach involves one or both. Practice on intentionally vulnerable environments such as CloudGoat, flAWS, EntraGoat, CNAPPGoat and PwnedLabs, which let you exploit and defend without getting fired.

Read real breach post-mortems. Pick up one foundational certification: CCSK is vendor-neutral and a great anchor, and the page lists AWS Security Specialty, Microsoft SC-100 and SC-500, and Google's Cloud Security Engineer as provider-specific options. Then join a community, because talking to practitioners is how you learn what is current.

== Is it different from regular cybersecurity?
layout: question
section: faq
Think about what you control on-prem, and what you do not control in the cloud.
notes:
This is the first question from the page's FAQ. Ask it and let the room answer before moving on.

== The principles are the same
layout: answer
section: faq
The implementation is wildly different. Identity takes over the job of the perimeter, configuration becomes the attack surface, and because everything is API-driven, defenders and attackers alike can automate all of it.
notes:
The principles are the same, but the page says the implementation is wildly different. On-prem, you control the network and physical access; in the cloud, you do not.

Identity takes over the job of the perimeter, configuration becomes the attack surface, and because everything is API-driven, defenders and attackers alike can automate all of it.

== What is CNAPP and do I need to care?
layout: question
section: faq
Think about whether you buy tools or are learning the field.
notes:
This is the second FAQ question. Ask it before showing the answer.

== It depends on your role
layout: answer
section: faq
CNAPP is the all-in-one platform category: CSPM, CWPP and CIEM in one tool. Operators buying tools will almost certainly evaluate CNAPP vendors. If you are learning, focus on posture, workload, entitlement and runtime, and the products will make sense.
notes:
CNAPP is the all-in-one cloud security platform category: CSPM, CWPP and CIEM in one tool. If you are an operator at a company buying tools, the page says you will almost certainly evaluate CNAPP vendors.

If you are learning, focus on the underlying concepts of posture, workload, entitlement and runtime, and the products will make sense.
